ipd: TX flow control, bounded TX reaping, own MSI-X vector + NAPI for TX completions

Measured 2026-11-11 (iperf3 over a 2x10G LACP bond, after ipd-rx-gro-napi):
ksoftirqd/0 at ~99% under bidirectional load, host TX flipping between
~200 Mb/s and ~1.6 Gb/s every few minutes while RX stays at 1.1-1.7 Gb/s,
TX-3 "Full" counter 146k, qdisc requeues 40k.

What the code did:

 * inno_enet_tx() returned NETDEV_TX_BUSY on a full ring without
   netif_stop_queue(), so the qdisc requeued and retried in a loop.
 * TX completions were reaped only in inno_ring_poll(), i.e. in the single
   NAPI context on CPU 0, after the RX pass, once per poll, unbounded.
   TCP only releases more data once the skbs it already queued are freed
   (TSQ), so the TX rate was tied to how soon the saturated RX softirq got
   around to reaping: this is the likely source of the bimodal TX rate
   (inferred from the code, not measured).
 * The TX-queue interrupt was steered to MSI-X vector 0 like RX
   (inno_alloc_ring(): msix_vector = MSIX_VECTOR_NAPI), although the device
   has 32 MSI-X table entries and a per-cause vector map (INTR_IMMR).

Changes:

 * Flow control.  After queuing a packet, stop the netdev queue when the
   ring cannot hold another worst-case packet (MAX_SKB_FRAGS + 3
   descriptors); the completion path wakes all stopped netdevs once a
   quarter of the ring is free.  The ring is shared by every netdev, so
   the wake walks sysport_devs[] (only when idev->tx_stopped is set).
   Stop/wake use the usual stop -> barrier -> re-check pairing so a wake
   cannot be lost.  NETDEV_TX_BUSY is still returned if two netdevs race
   for the last slots, but now with the queue stopped.
 * inno_tx_ring_poll() becomes inno_tx_ring_reap(tx, budget): bounded
   work, own lock (tx_reap_lock) instead of napi_lock so it no longer
   waits for a whole RX ring pass, and a sanity check of the CIDX read
   from the writeback area (a bad value made the walk endless).
 * Dedicated TX completion vector.  inno_intr_enable_msix() asks for one
   MSI-X entry more than the 8 userspace knows about (entry 8,
   "inno_irq-8-napi-tx"), the netdev TX ring interrupt is steered to it
   and handled by its own NAPI (inno_tx_napi_poll), by default on the
   second online CPU (irq_set_affinity_and_hint; change it through
   /proc/irq/N/smp_affinity).  The RX NAPI then does no TX work at all.
   Fallbacks: if the 9th vector cannot be allocated, with MSI/INTx, or
   with the module parameter tx_irq_split=0, TX completions stay on
   vector 0 as before (but bounded).  Until the new vector has fired
   once (tx_irq_seen) the RX NAPI keeps reaping too and the vector 0
   handler also masks/clears the TX cause, so a device that delivers the
   TX interrupt on vector 0 anyway behaves exactly as before, and one
   that does not deliver it at all still gets its completions reaped
   from the RX NAPI, the xmit path and the watchdog.
 * inno_enet_tx() reaps completions itself (trylock, max 64 packets) when
   less than a quarter of the ring is free, i.e. when the completion
   context is behind.
 * ndo_tx_timeout kicks the completion NAPI, so a queue that stayed
   stopped for watchdog_timeo (500 ms) recovers instead of only logging.

Not verified on hardware when written.

